  "scope": "The seventeen files of core/game/bots compiled unchanged on JDK 11 with Kotlin 1.8.20, JSON-simple 1.1.1 and the published ConstLib 1.4.28 (the Items names used checked against their suffixes), together with the original NodeList, NodeListIterator, Location, Vector, Direction, Item, IntType, Quests and Globals. Original text inserted into stand-ins at generation time: Player clear, finishClear, sendLogoutEvents, setPlaying, isPlaying, isArtificial, getName and getUsername, StringUtils.formatDisplayName, the Skills ids, the EquipmentContainer slots, the Log constants and ItemConfigParser.GE_PRICE. Everything else is a recording stand-in answering from the case: every other member of Player, Entity, Node, NPC and Scenery, the player's managers and containers, item definitions, pulses and pulse managers, pathfinding, RegionManager, interaction plugins and listeners, the Grand Exchange, consumables, RandomFunction, the session base class, ContentAPI's log/getWorldTicks/itemDefinition, the content scripts (CaseScript, Idler, Adventurer) and the pest control bots. Stand-ins declared in core.game.bots (a class Random and two random() extensions) are what the unchanged sources resolve `Random()` and `.random()` to, so those draws are recorded calls. kotlin.random.Random.nextInt(from, until) cannot be replaced or seeded: its two draws in randomizeLocationInRanges are read off the result, and the corpus only gives it ranges of one value. Name files are real files read by the original Scanner. The disconnection queue keeps what Player.clear writes and is compared as state. kotlinx.coroutines is a stand-in that keeps a launched block until the case runs it. Each case runs in a class loader of its own.",
